Found by the pre-release audit 2026-11-13 (fidelity-auditor; reproduced by the main agent).

One long-lived Environment, cached FileSystemLoader:
- {% macro m() %}{{ x }}{% endmacro %}{{ m() }}: 4.54 kB/render (3000 renders: RSS 22488 -> 36120 kB, main-agent probe at HEAD 1a9a608)
- with a 2000-row context the render's Context and the caller's data are kept: ~728 kB per request
- {% call %} / caller(): ~5 kB/render, context kept likewise
- from-imported macro library: ~5 kB/render (context not kept)
Any outer-name read inside the macro triggers it (a variable, another macro, a global, itself).

Cause: compiled macros are stored with $context->set_variable and capture $context; caller() closes over it the same way -> Context <-> macro cycle per render. Port-only (JS GC).

Fix needs a design choice: a macro can outlive its render (export / from-import / returned values), so a plain weaken of the captured Context may break those. The worker should propose the mapping; if it is a JS->Perl mapping decision it needs an ADR.

Done when: a regression test shows the Context of a render with a macro / caller() is freed, and the probe reads 0 kB/render.

Release blocker for long-running processes, same reasoning as the waterfall/async_iter card.

2026-11-17 main agent, re-diagnosed at HEAD 621ad19. The card describes one cycle; there are two.
Cycle A (as on the card): Context -> ctx{m} -> macro -> captured $context. Needs an outer-name read in the macro. Count probe (weak refs on every Context / Frame created): macro reading x, call/caller, from-import of a macro reading x each leave 1 Context + 1 Frame per render.
Cycle B (MISSING on the card): every macro captures $frame, because it swaps and restores that variable on each call. A macro stored in a frame that this variable reaches leaks without any outer-name read: '{% macro m() %}plain{% endmacro %}{% set f = m %}' leaves 1 Frame per render (0 Context); '{% from "lib" import m with context %}' inside a for loop leaves 3 Frames per render. Same shape on error paths (a macro that throws while called with a caller: $frame is not restored).
A plain weaken in the macro is not enough: macros of an imported library call each other through their Context, which must outlive the library's render.
Prototype (runtime patch of the emitted source, repo untouched) that brings every probe case to 0: (1) the macro captures a weak copy of $context and takes a strong one per call; (2) the importing Context keeps the library's Context alive (Template get_exported passes it as a third callback argument, the emitted import code stores it); (3) 'undef $frame' where a root or block sub is finished -- macros assign $frame before they read it, so the old value is unobservable. With k107 + k109 + these three loaded: prove -lr t/ green, 4623 tests in 21 files, 0 skipped, 89 TODO, the TODO set identical to the unpatched run. Real async deferral was not tested.
Open edges of the prototype, both at the Perl API, none reachable from a template: (a) a macro handed to the application's own Perl function during a render, stored there and called after the render, finds no Context if it reads outer names; (b) a direct Perl caller of Template get_exported (documented in the POD) gets macros whose Context is already gone -- closable by letting the returned macros hold their Context, not prototyped.
Alternative considered: keep the strong cycle and break it explicitly when the render ends. Has no trigger for a direct get_exported, and an escaped macro then renders silently wrong instead of failing.
Needs the maintainer's decision and an ADR (mapping of JS tracing GC to refcounting for macros).

DECISION 2026-11-17 (maintainer): fix the leak with the weak-capture direction and accept edge (a) as a documented limitation -- a macro that the application's own Perl code keeps and calls after the render that defined it has ended is not supported when it reads outer names. Edge (b), a direct Perl caller of Template get_exported, is to be closed: the returned macros keep their Context alive. Template-visible behaviour stays 1:1. To be recorded as ADR 0021.

